      trivia: "【Cigar Names as a Chronicle of Exile】The “1959” of Exodus 1959 is the year of the Cuban Revolution, and the “Exodus” is that of the Book of Exodus; Noventa (ninety) marks the family's ninety years in tobacco. Toraño's line names amount, almost in their entirety, to a timeline of the family's history.\n【Kin to the Partagás House】Santiago Toraño's daughter married Ramón Cifuentes of Partagás — tying the family by blood to one of the great names of the Cuban cigar.\n【The Factories Sold before the Brands】The factories in Estelí and Danlí went to STG at the end of 2009, the brands to General Cigar in 2014. As General is itself part of STG, factories and brands were reunited in the same group five years apart.\n【What Was Bought Was the Trademarks, Not the Company】In the 2014 transaction only the brands were acquired; the Toraño Family Cigar Company was closed and its staff did not move to General — a rare case of a century-old family business vanishing with only its trademarks left behind.\n【A Nicaraguan Cigar Laced with Pennsylvania Leaf】Vault D-042 is an unusual work in which makers of Cuban stock blended US Pennsylvania leaf into the filler — the fruit of a chance encounter on a buying trip in 1998."
